+# Keep background jobs in Postgres
+
+For whoever changes background jobs or operates their queue. Jobs live in Postgres through procrastinate because
+Redis evicted receipt jobs silently, leaving members without receipts and volunteers dependent on one operator.
+
+## Context
+
+In the March 4–9 discussion in [issue 12](../../notes/issue-12.md), members reported about 300 missing receipts since
+Saturday. The email provider had received nothing since Saturday at 23:10; Tom traced the loss to the Redis box
+running out of memory and evicting queued Celery receipt jobs. No error was logged. Nadia resent the receipts by
+hand from the orders table, taking most of her Tuesday.
+
+Redis served only the job queue, and Tom was the only volunteer who knew how to restore it. Postgres was already
+backed up nightly and every volunteer could query it. The choice needed to protect queued work, keep the services
+volunteers operate manageable, and avoid a gap between committing a pantry order and queuing its receipt.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Keep Celery and Redis, adding persistence and a memory alarm**: the least work that week. Tom also said Redis
+  would need sizing for the worst week. Rejected because it retained a separate service whose recovery depended on
+  one volunteer.
+- **Use Celery with its database backend**: considered as a way to move jobs to the database; rejected because Ana
+  reported that Celery's documentation marked it experimental.
+- **Use procrastinate with Postgres**: chosen for its ability to enqueue a job in the same transaction as the pantry
+  order, using a database the volunteers already backed up and could inspect.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep background jobs in Postgres using procrastinate, replacing Celery and Redis. Enqueue a receipt job in the same
+transaction as the pantry order that queues it, so committing the order and recording its job succeed or fail
+together. This addresses the queue loss and the commit-to-enqueue gap without retaining Redis solely for jobs.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Volunteers can inspect queued work in the database they already know, and queue recovery no longer requires
+restoring a separate Redis service. The migration removes Redis from the job stack; Postgres now carries the queue's
+storage and workload as well as the app's data, and its availability and recovery also affect background jobs.
+Changes to order creation must preserve the shared transaction when queuing receipts.
+
+The transaction guarantee concerns recording the job, not successful email delivery. This checkout contains only
+the [receipt job stub](../../larder/receipts.py), not the enqueue transaction, worker setup or recovery tooling;
+those operational details and the transaction wiring cannot be verified here.
